{
  "rules": [
    "Near-term (days 1–5): model is slightly high on average (~+0.3 to +1.5 €/t). Apply a small downward bias correction early in the horizon, especially when the recent history is flat-to-down.",
    "Mid-horizon (days ~6–18): model tends to overshoot and is too volatile versus realized prices (predicts sharper swings). Damp forecast deviations toward a smoother path (shrink daily changes / pull toward a short moving average).",
    "Rally regime (strong upward trend in outcomes): model underestimates the upside after the first ~2–3 weeks (days ~20–30). Add a positive trend continuation term late-horizon when the last 10–15 days show persistent higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Spike handling: after upward spikes, the model often mean-reverts too quickly (predicts pullbacks that are larger/earlier than realized). Reduce mean-reversion strength following breakouts; allow elevated levels to persist longer.",
    "Late-horizon turning points: model sometimes predicts a late drop that does not materialize (e.g., forecasts falling into the high-60s while outcomes stay low/mid-70s). When momentum is positive, cap late-horizon downside adjustments unless history shows clear rollover (multiple consecutive lower closes)."
  ]
}
